Z||000000||Jaei Sun||06-27-2001||05:34 PM||||I'm guessing this product is like the infamous "TORNADO" intake POS... but does anyone know about these exhaust tips??<P>They're called the Turbulator Power Exhaust tips.  They have to be welded onto the muffler and they look to have what looks to be like a little throttle body valve on them.  Taken from the cat:<P>"Patented self-adjusting control valve responds to the engine's RPM load and temperature to equalize pressure and regulate exhaust flow.  During high exhaust flow, internal valve opens ot let engine gasses escape freely.  At lower RPM, valve closes to regulate exhaust flow and improve engine performance."<P>any ideas info.  To me it doesn't make any sense, wouldn't you want free flowing exhaust all the time??<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://home.mn.rr.com/fierojaei/trans_fiero_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||WampusCat||06-27-2001||06:05 PM||||This is from Subaru's website discussing their new 3.0 flat six in the VDC and L.L.Bean wagons:  <BR>"A mechanical valve in the rear muffler varies muffler volume to help enhance engine power in different speed ranges. At lower engine speeds (around-town driving, for example), the valve remains closed to provide a smaller-volume muffler chamber. The resulting level of exhaust back-pressure enhances power at these speeds. When exhaust pressure reaches about 22 psi -- which occurs at about 2,400 rpm -- the valve opens to allow exhaust gas through an additional chamber. The resulting increased muffler volume reduces exhaust back-pressure, enhancing power at higher speeds. Muffler valve operation does not affect the noise level."  <BR>Sounds like a similar system/idea.||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||06-27-2001||06:52 PM||||The more back pressure would increase torque and at higher speed, when it frees the exhaust flow, it would give you more HP. Right? (I know nothing about the product, but the theory would make sense if it actually works that way.)||||reg||1
